                    coach_default:          `Decision logged.`,
                    coach_r1_layer_right:   `<strong>Right — the value is in the data and analytics layer.</strong> Your sensors create the data the whole market runs on, but you capture none of the €45M of recurring revenue that sits on top of it. That gap between value created and value captured is the entire game.`,
                    coach_r1_hardware:      `<strong>Your engineers just made the best sensor in Europe — congratulations.</strong> But PlantSense gives the sensor away to win the €90-a-month subscription, and your hardware margin is sliding toward 22%. You would be innovating in the one layer the market is commoditising. Where is value actually <em>captured</em>?`,
                    coach_r1_connectivity:  `<strong>The pipes are not where the durable value sits.</strong> Connectivity is a thin, increasingly standardised layer. The recurring economics are one layer up, in the analytics that turn the data into uptime customers will pay for.`,
                    coach_r1_nowhere:       `<strong>If value were captured evenly, PlantSense would not exist.</strong> The whole point of their move is that recurring analytics revenue concentrates value in one defensible layer. Pretending it is spread out is how you keep leaking it.`,
                    coach_r1_diag_sharp:    `<strong>Sharp diagnosis.</strong> You located capture in the data/analytics layer AND named the analytics models as the exposed, weak-appropriability layer. That is exactly the lens that tells you where to innovate and what to defend.`,
                    coach_r1_exp_hardware:  `<strong>The hardware is not your exposure — it is your least exposed asset.</strong> Three granted patents and 16 years of reliability protect it. The unguarded door is the analytics layer, which has no patents and can be copied in 6–9 months.`,
                    coach_r1_exp_none:      `<strong>That's how you get eaten.</strong> Calling PlantSense noise is exactly the complacency that lets a fast follower own the data layer. The exposure is real, it is the analytics, and it is measured in months.`,
                    coach_r1_lowclarity:    `<strong>A blurry create-vs-capture diagnosis dooms the rest.</strong> If you cannot separate the value you generate from the value you keep, every later decision defends the wrong layer. Get the distinction sharp first.`,
                    coach_r2_buy:           `<strong>Buying the Paris start-up is the balanced play.</strong> €28M and live in 9 months — fast enough to land inside the imitation window while keeping the data relationship and the upside in-house. Now integrate it before the key people walk.`,
                    coach_r2_build:         `<strong>Build gives you full IP — in 18 months.</strong> But in 18 months PlantSense has the head start, the network effects and four of your top accounts. The control is real; the clock is the problem. What holds the base while you build?`,
                    coach_r2_partner:       `<strong>You're live in 5 months — impressive.</strong> But your cloud partner now owns the data relationship and takes 35% of every euro forever. In 24 months they have the network effects and you're a hardware vendor again, on a thinner margin. You captured speed and ceded the moat.`,
                    coach_r2_focus_hw:      `<strong>You've pointed the innovation budget at the commoditising layer.</strong> Out-engineering a sensor PlantSense gives away for free does not capture recurring value. Concentrate the effort on the analytics platform where the margin actually lives.`,
                    coach_r2_focus_split:   `<strong>Splitting the envelope dilutes the one bet that matters.</strong> Spreading money evenly across hardware and platform under-funds the data layer just as the window is closing. Concentrate where value is captured.`,
                    coach_r2_nointerim:     `<strong>No interim move leaves the base undefended.</strong> The two paused pilot customers are evaluating PlantSense right now. With nothing to hold them, you may lose the installed base that is the source of your data-layer value before the platform even ships.`,
                    coach_r3_outcome:       `<strong>Outcome-based pricing captures value where it is created.</strong> Pay-per-uptime ties your revenue to the result the data delivers — the deepest alignment with the customer and the hardest model for a hardware-giveaway rival to match.`,
                    coach_r3_subscription:  `<strong>A subscription on your installed base is the right direction.</strong> Recurring €/machine/month revenue mirrors PlantSense but on customers you already serve. Outcome-based would capture even more, but this is a solid, defensible model.`,
                    coach_r3_onetime:       `<strong>You launched a subscription and kept selling hardware one-time.</strong> Now two business models fight each other, and your own discounting cannibalises the recurring revenue. Which model is the company actually committing to?`,
                    coach_r3_datamoat:      `<strong>Data network effects are the moat that compounds.</strong> The more machines on the platform, the better the models, the harder any customer is to win away — and PlantSense cannot reach your installed base. This defence gets stronger every month, not weaker.`,
                    coach_r3_switching:     `<strong>Switching costs through deep workflow integration are a durable defence.</strong> Once the bundle is wired into the customer's operations, leaving is expensive. Pair it with data network effects and the moat compounds.`,
                    coach_r3_patents:       `<strong>You filed a dozen patents on the algorithms.</strong> By the time they grant, the architecture has moved twice and competitors have engineered around them. In a software-paced market the moat is data and switching costs, not the patent office. Which mechanism actually compounds?`,
                    coach_r3_brand:         `<strong>Brand cannot out-shout a 6x marketing budget.</strong> Trying to win on reputation alone against a cash-rich incumbent is the one fight you are funded to lose. A structural moat — data, switching costs — does not need a bigger megaphone.`,
                    coach_r3_nodefense:     `<strong>You named a moat and starved it.</strong> Data lock-in and integration only compound if you fund them. With almost no investment in the defensive mechanism, the platform is excellent and copyable — nothing stops a competitor in 7 months.`,
                    coach_r3_misaligned:    `<strong>Your sales force is still on one-time hardware quotas.</strong> Launching recurring pricing while incentives reward transactional deals guarantees the new model is cannibalised from inside. Align the incentives or the architecture is theatre.`,
                    coach_r4_fast:          `<strong>A staged launch beats the imitation clock.</strong> The interim move holds the base now and the full platform lands inside the window — defensibility is a race, and you are running it. A good fast answer is worth more than a perfect late one.`,
                    coach_r4_credible:      `<strong>A single clean launch in nine months is defensible</strong> — but with no interim move, the two paused pilots and the four exposed top accounts are unprotected until you ship. It can work; it leaves less margin for error than a staged launch.`,
                    coach_r4_late:          `<strong>Your build is sound — in 18 months.</strong> But in 18 months four of your top ten accounts have signed exclusive deals with PlantSense and 28% of your revenue's data is locked away forever. What do you ship in the next two quarters to hold the base?`,
                    coach_r4_adjusted:      `<strong>An imitation-adjusted ROI is an honest ROI.</strong> Discounting returns by the probability and speed of being copied is exactly what the board needs to trust the number — and it forces the strategy to earn its first-mover claim rather than assume it.`,
                    coach_r4_gross:         `<strong>Presenting the full €45M as guaranteed will not survive scrutiny.</strong> A board that has just watched PlantSense launch knows first-mover advantage is not free. An un-discounted ROI reads as wishful, and it undermines the credible parts of your case.`,
                    coach_r4_vague:         `<strong>A vision with no numbers is not a defendable ROI.</strong> Platform-leadership narrative is fine as framing, but the board chartered this team for a committed date and a defendable return. Avoiding the math reads as not having done it.`,
                    coach_r4_answer_moat:   `<strong>That is a board-ready answer.</strong> A compounding data moat plus switching costs on an installed base PlantSense cannot reach — defended with an honest, imitation-adjusted ROI and a date you have already started hitting. That is what stops them.`,
                    coach_r4_answer_patent: `<strong>Patents are the wrong headline answer here.</strong> They grant too slowly for a software-paced fight and competitors design around them. Lead with the data moat and switching costs; keep patents as a secondary, not the wall.`,
                    coach_r4_answer_speed:  `<strong>Speed without structure is a moat that evaporates.</strong> Out-running them this year means nothing if there is no lock-in next year. The board's question is what stops them <em>structurally</em> — speed alone is not an answer.`,
                    coach_r4_answer_hope:   `<strong>Loyalty is not an isolating mechanism.</strong> Reliability reputation is real but it does not stop a customer signing a cheaper subscription. 'They'll stay loyal' is precisely the assumption a fast follower is built to break.`,

